+## Release 1.6.7
+
+Release 1.6.7 corrects what the client's review of 1.6.6 named about completeness, the category mapping and the
+UPCs of carried rows. Each new test was first run on the frozen 1.6.6 collector (commit `39cc61f`, its tree
+extracted byte for byte, the 1.6.7 tests copied in) on Ubuntu 24.04 (the owner's Azure VM), and failed there by its
+own assertion; it passes on 1.6.7. Two tests check functions that 1.6.7 adds; on 1.6.6 they fail because the
+function does not exist (named below). The requests a run makes are unchanged; what changes is how its listings and
+rows are judged and named, and which saved values a carried row takes.
+
+| Change | Until 1.6.6 | Where |
+|---|---|---|
+| A partitioned listing is complete only when every child category ended complete and their distinct products balance its reported total. A child that ended on a moving total, a repeated or wrong page or its result window keeps the listing incomplete even when the identities balance, and the reason names the child; a child left by a limit of the run makes it `CAPPED`; a partitioned child without a child category of its own, or a partition with no child recorded, is incomplete | A partition was `DONE` whenever its distinct identities balanced and no child was still being read, whatever the children's own ends (also with a child cut by a limit of the run) | `collection/walmart.py` (`listing_outcomes`); `tests/unit/test_completeness_167.py`: 7 tests fail on 1.6.6 (`'DONE' == 'INCOMPLETE'`, `'DONE' == 'CAPPED'`); the control (every child complete) passes on both |
+| A CSV row read only in part (through its configured listings, `--subcategory`, or with listings its category page names that the run did not choose) is `CAPPED`, never `DONE`, and the reason says so; `coverage-report` lists it in `csv_rows_read_in_part` and its coverage is not complete | The row was `DONE`, and with every CSV row planned the run's coverage was reported complete | `collection/walmart.py` (`row_read_in_part`, `collect_node`), `collection/reconcile.py`; `tests/integration/test_category_mapping.py::test_a_row_read_only_through_its_configured_listings_is_never_complete` (fails on 1.6.6: `(['NODE_DONE'], True)`; its control, a whole read, is complete on both); `tests/unit/test_completeness_167.py::test_a_row_read_through_configured_listings_or_with_listings_not_chosen_is_read_in_part` (new function) |
+| `coverage-report` gives each top-level listing its effective outcome (`effective_status`, `effective_reason`: a partition judged through its child categories, as the collector judges it) and each CSV row its verdict (`csv_rows`: planned, read in part, listings, complete, why not; `csv_rows_complete`). `coverage_complete` is true only when every CSV row is complete | A partitioned listing stayed `PARTITIONED` in the report, so no CSV row with a partition could be reported complete, even when the collector called the row `DONE` | `collection/reconcile.py`; `tests/integration/test_coverage_and_assortment.py::test_the_coverage_report_judges_a_partition_through_its_children_and_gives_each_csv_row_its_verdict` and `::test_a_partition_whose_total_moved_leaves_its_csv_row_incomplete_in_the_report` (fail on 1.6.6: no `effective_status`) |
+| The agreed category mapping: a listing read by its path takes the name the CSV row's category page gave it, from the category pages runs of the store stored (`SUBCATEGORIES_FOUND` events; no request). The row's own category page wins over another row's, and a replay uses only pages stored before its source run. The run's `SUBCATEGORIES_CONFIGURED` event names the run and event the name came from, or says there was none (the listing's own page's name is then used). A carried row takes the category page's name too; its lineage keeps the name it had, the rule and the source record's hash (`historical.renamed_by_mapping`), and its own crawl date | A listing read by its path took the name its own result page states. Live: listing `1115193_1071967_1630538` was "Fabric Softeners" in L7 and L10 (its category page) and "Fabric Softener" in L8 and L12 (its own page) | `collection/navigation.py` (new), `collection/walmart.py` (`collect_node`, `_page`, `h_assemble`), `collection/retention.py` (`carry_forward`); `tests/integration/test_category_mapping.py`: 3 tests fail on 1.6.6 (`['Milk Products'] == ['Milk']`; `None == {'976759_9176907': None}`), 1 checks the new function (`navigation.category_page_names`) |
